    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nIn this essay, Virginia Rose La Puma interviews experts including judges and law professors to reveal that the use of neuroscience as evidence in the courtroom is challenged on multiple levels, not the least of which involves juries and potential prejudice. She further recommended a solution: standardized jury instructions whenever juries must\r\nconsider neurological testimony or evidence, and shifting the focus away from assigning guilt and toward predicting future actions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
